Subtype Commands APIDs Examples 5 10 172, 188, 204, 220, 236, 268 (CSW) and 812, 1164, 1212, 1532 (EPD, PHI, RPW, SWA) ZCD00505 "Enable Event Report Generation", ZIA58058 "SWA_TC_EVENT_EN" 6 10 same ZCD00506 "Disable Event Report Generation" 128 1 172 ZCD00580 "Clear Critical Event Log" 129 1 172 ZCD00581 "Downlink Crit. Event Log" 131 6 CSW ZCD00583 "Report Enabled Event Packets" 133 7 CSW and 1164 (PHI) ZCD00585 "Report Disabled Event Packets" <p>All 35 have <code>CCF_ACK</code> = 9 (acceptance and completion reports) and none is flagged critical.<sup>75</sup> In CDF, <code>ZCD00505</code> is an 8-bit counter "Nb EIDs" (PTC 3, PFC 4) with group size 1, followed by the repeated 16-bit "EID" (PTC 2, PFC 16) - the ICD layout of section 8.3. The SWA command <code>ZIA58058</code> instead starts with an 8-bit spare, then an 8-bit count and a 16-bit unsigned event ID (PTC 3, PFC 12), showing that instruments keep their own variants.<sup>75</sup></p> <p>The private table reports are variable packets (VPD): TM(5,134) on APID 163 is a 16-bit count followed by repeated 16-bit EIDs; TM(5,130) is an 8-bit SGM ID, a 16-bit count, then repeated groups of PID (8 bits), EID (16 bits) and occurrence count (16 bits). They use APIDs of packet category 3, "Table generation".<sup>76</sup><sup>68</sup></p> SQL used <pre><code>-- solo-2025-09-16.sqlite (and solo-2020-04-16.sqlite for the 2020 counts)
SELECT CASE WHEN p BETWEEN 10 AND 16 THEN 'CSW' WHEN p BETWEEN 30 AND 32 THEN 'SSMM'
            WHEN p BETWEEN 40 AND 41 THEN 'STR' WHEN p BETWEEN 50 AND 104 THEN 'payload'
            WHEN p BETWEEN 112 AND 127 THEN 'EGSE' END grp,
       COUNT(*), SUM(st='1'), SUM(st='2'), SUM(st='3'), SUM(st='4'), SUM(CAST(st AS INT) &gt; 100)
  FROM (SELECT CAST(PID_APID AS INT) &gt;&gt; 4 p, PID_STYPE st FROM pid WHERE PID_TYPE = '5')
 GROUP BY 1;
SELECT PID_STYPE, PID_EVENT, COUNT(*) FROM pid WHERE PID_TYPE = '5' GROUP BY 1, 2;
SELECT * FROM pic WHERE PIC_TYPE = '5';
-- event ID convention: top two bits = subtype - 1
SELECT PID_STYPE, SUM(((CAST(PID_PI1_VAL AS INT) &gt;&gt; 14) + 1) = CAST(PID_STYPE AS INT)), COUNT(*)
  FROM pid WHERE PID_TYPE = '5' AND PID_STYPE IN ('1','2','3','4')
   AND (CAST(PID_APID AS INT) &gt;&gt; 4) BETWEEN 10 AND 16 GROUP BY 1;
SELECT COUNT(*), SUM(TRIM(PID_EVID) &lt;&gt; '') FROM pid;
SELECT * FROM txf WHERE TXF_NUMBR = 'CCDT0004TX';
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT PLF_SPID) FROM plf WHERE PLF_NAME = 'NCD05011';   -- 2 322
-- CSW event packets (subtypes 1-4) with NCD05011 in PLF (2 239) or first in VPD (68)
SELECT COUNT(*), SUM(EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM plf WHERE PLF_NAME = 'NCD05011' AND PLF_SPID = p.PID_SPID))
  FROM pid p WHERE PID_TYPE = '5' AND PID_STYPE IN ('1','2','3','4')
   AND (CAST(PID_APID AS INT) &gt;&gt; 4) BETWEEN 10 AND 16;
SELECT p.PID_DESCR, t.TPCF_SIZE FROM pid p JOIN tpcf t ON t.TPCF_SPID = p.PID_SPID
 WHERE p.PID_TYPE = '5' AND CAST(t.TPCF_SIZE AS INT) - 16 &gt; 40;
SELECT CCF_CNAME, CCF_DESCR, CCF_STYPE, CCF_APID, CCF_ACK, CCF_CRITICAL FROM ccf WHERE CCF_TYPE = '5';
SELECT OCP_TYPE, COUNT(*) FROM ocp GROUP BY 1;
-- swa-idb.sqlite
SELECT PID_STYPE, PID_EVENT, COUNT(*) FROM pid WHERE PID_TYPE = '5' GROUP BY 1, 2;
</code></pre>

Report Definitions APIDs Identification (PIC) TM(6,6) 289 (219 variable, 70 fixed) 24 generic: memory ID at octet 16, 16 bits; four APIDs (1961, 1965, 1976, 1977) add the start address at octet 18, 32 bits TM(6,10) 20 20 no extra field: one definition per APID <p>Source: PID, PIC.<sup>104</sup> For the OBC dump APID 169 (process 10, category 9) there are 38 TM(6,6) definitions, one per memory ID, each a variable packet whose VPD structure is memory ID, start address, a block-length repeat counter and the repeated 32-bit data words - for example SPID 19501 Memory Dump A_PM_PROM.<sup>104</sup> The 70 fixed dump definitions decode specific memory contents as ordinary parameters: SPID 64100 "Dump_RMH_ATPTR_CROME_REG_A" (memory 101, address 5642) ends with the 32-bit parameter <code>NCDX0200</code> "RMH_ATPTR_CROME_REG_A", matching the dump command <code>ZCDX0200</code> and the load command <code>ZCDX0100</code> above.<sup>104</sup><sup>103</sup></p> <p>Inference: the dedicated dump APIDs</p> <p>62 of the 70 fixed dump definitions - OBC register, log and RAM-table dumps such as SPID 64100, SSMM memory-module error logs and status, and star-tracker settable parameters (APIDs 1976 and 1977) - sit on APIDs 1961, 1965, 1976 and 1977 (process IDs 122-123, in the range the ICD allocates to EGSE), whereas the OBC's own dump APID is 169.<sup>99</sup><sup>104</sup> How dumps reach these APIDs on ground is not stated in the available sources. The other 8 fixed definitions are SoloHI dumps on APID 1321 (process 82), with memory IDs <code>0xE000</code>-<code>0xE007</code>, inside the <code>0xE0--</code> range the ICD reserves for SoloHI.<sup>85</sup><sup>104</sup></p> <p>The OBC check report SPID 14170 "Memory Check Using Absolute Address Report" is a fixed 28-octet packet:<sup>104</sup></p> <p>ISO or CRC?</p> <p>The ICD says TC(6,9) uses the CRC 16 of its Annex 6, and the TM(6,10) parameter is named "OBCHP_Computed CRC"; yet the MIB's extended description of <code>ZCD00609</code> (<code>CCF_DESCR2</code>) speaks of an "'ISO' checksum report".<sup>91</sup><sup>103</sup> The available sources do not resolve the discrepancy; the ICD is the more authoritative of the two.</p> SQL used <pre><code>-- solo-2025-09-16.sqlite
SELECT CCF_STYPE, COUNT(*), COUNT(DISTINCT CCF_APID) FROM ccf WHERE CCF_TYPE = '6' GROUP BY 1;
SELECT CCF_TYPE, CCF_STYPE, CCF_ACK, CCF_CRITICAL, COUNT(*) FROM ccf
 WHERE CCF_TYPE = '6' GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4;
SELECT PAS_ALTXT, PAS_ALVAL FROM pas WHERE PAS_NUMBR = 'CCDT0037TX' ORDER BY CAST(PAS_ALVAL AS INT);
-- modelling style of each TC(6,2)
WITH s AS (SELECT f.CCF_CNAME n, MAX(c.CPC_PTC = '7') os, MAX(d.CDF_GRPSIZE &gt; 0) rep
             FROM ccf f JOIN cdf d ON d.CDF_CNAME = f.CCF_CNAME
             LEFT JOIN cpc c ON c.CPC_NAME = d.CDF_PNAME
            WHERE f.CCF_TYPE = '6' AND f.CCF_STYPE = '2' GROUP BY 1)
SELECT CASE WHEN os THEN 'octet-string' WHEN rep THEN 'repeater' ELSE 'fixed' END, COUNT(*)
  FROM s GROUP BY 1;
SELECT d.*, c.CPC_DESCR, c.CPC_PTC, c.CPC_PFC FROM cdf d LEFT JOIN cpc c ON c.CPC_NAME = d.CDF_PNAME
 WHERE d.CDF_CNAME IN ('ZCD00602', 'ZCDX0602', 'ZCDX0100', 'ZCD0068C', 'ZCDX0200');
SELECT PID_STYPE, COUNT(*), COUNT(DISTINCT PID_APID), SUM(PID_TPSD = '-1')
  FROM pid WHERE PID_TYPE = '6' GROUP BY 1;
SELECT PID_APID, PID_PI1_VAL, COUNT(*) FROM pid            -- where the 70 fixed dumps are
 WHERE PID_TYPE = '6' AND PID_STYPE = '6' AND PID_TPSD = '-1' GROUP BY 1, 2;
SELECT * FROM pic WHERE PIC_TYPE = '6';
SELECT * FROM vpd WHERE VPD_TPSD = '19501' ORDER BY CAST(VPD_POS AS INT);
SELECT PLF_NAME, PLF_OFFBY FROM plf WHERE PLF_SPID IN ('14170', '64100');
</code></pre> <p>The April 2020 release had essentially the same command set (85, 78 and 29 definitions of subtypes 2, 5 and 9) and 278 TM(6,6) definitions.<sup>105</sup> The SWA instrument database contributes one TC(6,2), (6,5), (6,9) and (6,129) each, one TM(6,10) and 53 TM(6,6) definitions, identified by the same PIC rule (memory ID at octet 16, 16 bits).<sup>106</sup></p>

SQL used <pre><code>-- solo-2025-09-16.sqlite (MIB__20250916M25925PFMS001_SOL)
SELECT CCF_TYPE, CCF_STYPE, COUNT(*), group_concat(CCF_CNAME) FROM ccf
 WHERE CCF_TYPE = '11' GROUP BY 1, 2;                                -- all APID 172
SELECT d.CDF_CNAME, d.CDF_ELTYPE, d.CDF_ELLEN, d.CDF_BIT, d.CDF_GRPSIZE, d.CDF_PNAME, d.CDF_VALUE,
       c.CPC_DESCR, c.CPC_PTC, c.CPC_PFC, c.CPC_CATEG, c.CPC_PAFREF, c.CPC_PRFREF, c.CPC_DEFVAL
  FROM cdf d LEFT JOIN cpc c ON c.CPC_NAME = d.CDF_PNAME
 WHERE d.CDF_CNAME IN (SELECT CCF_CNAME FROM ccf WHERE CCF_TYPE = '11')
 ORDER BY d.CDF_CNAME, CAST(d.CDF_BIT AS INT);
SELECT f.PRF_NUMBR, f.PRF_DESCR, v.PRV_MINVAL, v.PRV_MAXVAL FROM prf f JOIN prv v ON v.PRV_NUMBR = f.PRF_NUMBR
 WHERE f.PRF_NUMBR IN ('VCD00164','VCD00265','VCD00269','VCD00170','VCD00185','VCD00186');
SELECT * FROM pas WHERE PAS_NUMBR = 'CCDT0022TC';
SELECT * FROM pid WHERE PID_TYPE = '11';                             -- SPIDs 14174..14176, APID 163
SELECT * FROM vpd WHERE VPD_TPSD IN ('14174','14175','14176') ORDER BY 1, CAST(VPD_POS AS INT);
-- default sub-schedules
SELECT CCF_SUBSCHEDID, CCF_SUBSYS, COUNT(*), substr(MIN(CCF_CNAME), 1, 3) FROM ccf GROUP BY 1, 2;
SELECT CSF_SUBSCHEDID, COUNT(*) FROM csf GROUP BY 1;
SELECT CSF_IFTT, COUNT(*) FROM csf GROUP BY 1;                       -- B 3354, N 50
-- protection loop
SELECT c.CCF_CNAME, c.CCF_DESCR, (SELECT group_concat(CDF_VALUE, ' ') FROM
       (SELECT CDF_VALUE FROM cdf WHERE CDF_CNAME = c.CCF_CNAME ORDER BY CAST(CDF_BIT AS INT)))
  FROM ccf c WHERE c.CCF_CNAME LIKE 'ZCSX00%' AND CCF_TYPE = '11';
SELECT d.CDF_CNAME, (SELECT CDF_VALUE FROM cdf e WHERE e.CDF_CNAME = d.CDF_CNAME AND e.CDF_PNAME = 'PCD038B1'),
       d.CDF_VALUE, t.CCF_DESCR
  FROM cdf d JOIN ccf t ON t.CCF_CNAME = d.CDF_VALUE
 WHERE d.CDF_PNAME = 'PCD13013' AND d.CDF_CNAME LIKE 'ZCSX00%';
SELECT SDF_SQNAME, group_concat(SDF_VALUE, ',') FROM
       (SELECT * FROM sdf WHERE SDF_ELEMID IN ('ZCD00B01','ZCD00B02') AND SDF_PNAME = 'PCD0B012'
         ORDER BY SDF_SQNAME, CAST(SDF_POS AS INT)) GROUP BY SDF_SQNAME;
-- verification stages (cvp/cvs have generic columns COL1..COLn in ICD order)
SELECT p.COL1, p.COL3, s.COL2, s.COL3, s.COL4, s.COL5 FROM cvp p LEFT JOIN cvs s ON s.COL1 = p.COL3
 WHERE p.COL1 IN ('ZCD00B04', 'INSERTTT', 'ZCD00B01', 'ZCSX0021');
-- release comparison
ATTACH 'solo-2020-04-16.sqlite' AS o;
SELECT CCF_CNAME FROM ccf WHERE CCF_TYPE = '11' AND CCF_CNAME NOT IN (SELECT CCF_CNAME FROM o.ccf);
SELECT CCF_CNAME FROM ccf WHERE CCF_CNAME LIKE 'ZCSX%' AND CCF_CNAME NOT IN (SELECT CCF_CNAME FROM o.ccf);
SELECT CDF_CNAME, group_concat(CDF_VALUE, ' ') FROM (SELECT * FROM o.cdf WHERE CDF_CNAME LIKE 'ZCSX00%'
       ORDER BY CDF_CNAME, CAST(CDF_BIT AS INT)) GROUP BY 1;                -- 2020: N1 = 3
SELECT CSS_SQNAME, CSS_ELEMID FROM css WHERE CSS_ELEMID IN ('ZCSX0033', 'ZCSX0042', 'ZCSX0021');
SELECT CSS_ELEMID FROM o.css WHERE CSS_SQNAME = 'ADMF730A';                 -- 2020 loads ZCSX0033
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM o.csf WHERE CSF_NAME LIKE 'ADMF740%' OR CSF_NAME GLOB 'AI?C805*';  -- 0
</code></pre> <ol> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, Telemetry and telecommand packet utilization, §6.11.1.1 General, PDF p. 168. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.1.2 Time-based scheduling subservice, PDF pp. 168-169. ↩↩↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.2 Service layout, PDF p. 169. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.3.1 Accessibility, application process, and notes 1 to 3, PDF p. 169. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.4.1 Capability, PDF p. 170. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.4.2 General, requirements a to e, PDF pp. 170-171. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.4.3.1 Status, PDF p. 171. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.4.3.2 and §6.11.4.3.3, PDF pp. 171-172. ↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.4.4 Reset the time-based schedule, PDF p. 172. ↩↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.4.5 Insert activities, requirements a to e, PDF pp. 172-173. ↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.4.5 requirements f to k, PDF pp. 173-174. ↩↩↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.4.6 Schedule execution logic and notes 1 to 3, PDF pp. 174-175. ↩↩↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.5.1 Time-based sub-schedules, PDF p. 175. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.5.2.1 and §6.11.5.2.2 Enable and disable time-based sub-schedules, PDF pp. 175-176. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.5.2.3 Report the status of each time-based sub-schedule, PDF p. 177. ↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.6.1 Time-based scheduling groups, PDF p. 177. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.6.2 Creating and deleting time-based scheduling groups, PDF pp. 178-179. ↩↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.6.3 Enabling, disabling and reporting groups, PDF pp. 179-181. ↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.7.1 Time-based schedule summary report, PDF pp. 181-182. ↩</p> </li> <li> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-41C, §6.11.7.2 Time-based schedule detail report and note 4, PDF pp. 182-183. ↩↩</p>