Aspect SCOS-2000 ground checks (OCF/OCP) PUS-C ST[12] parameter monitoring Solar Orbiter service 12 Where and when control centre, every valid sample received on board, every monitoring interval on board, interval in application cycles Defined by MIB import; limit values changeable on line<sup>20</sup> ground requests TC[12,5] etc. at run time TC(12,5) add/modify, TC(12,7) modify Check types soft/hard limit, status set, delta, status consistency, event limit, expected value (mask), optional delta limit or expected value (mask) Severity levels soft and hard in one set separate definitions (e.g. warning and danger) one limit pair per entry, an EID per limit Conditional checking <code>OCP_RLCHK = OCP_VALPAR</code>, first applicable set wins check validity condition: (param AND mask) = expected Boolean validity parameter (0 = always) Persistence <code>OCF_NBCHCK</code> consecutive violating valid samples repetition number, both directions <code>Rep</code>, 1–255, both directions At the limit equal is not OOL low ≤ value ≤ high is success not stated Result OOL display, events, OOL archive (EDDS, TDRS) events; TM[12,12] transitions; TM[12,11] OOL list events; TM(12,12); TM(12,11) <p>Sources: ICD rules,<sup>7</sup><sup>4</sup> ECSS-E-ST-70-41C clauses 6.12.3.2–6.12.3.12,<sup>25</sup><sup>26</sup><sup>27</sup><sup>30</sup><sup>31</sup> and the Solar Orbiter TM-TC and Packet Structure ICD.<sup>34</sup><sup>35</sup> Earlier ESA requirements show the same model. The Herschel/Planck operations interface requirements ask that a parameter fail (or pass again) the given number of times before an event is generated, both when going out of limits and when returning within limits.<sup>39</sup></p> <p>The neutral data model of ECSS-E-ST-70-31C covers both worlds. A limit check carries domain-specific limit sets (AIV, Operations, Software), each selected by a validity condition expression, with upper and lower danger and warning limits and a corrective activity for each. Delta checks have maximum and minimum thresholds, expected-state checks have lists of values, and status consistency checks have a command argument or expression as reference.<sup>32</sup><sup>33</sup></p> <p>Inference</p> <p>ECSS-E-ST-70-31C warning and danger limits correspond naturally to SCOS-2000 soft and hard limits, and its "first true validity condition applies" matches the SCOS-2000 "first applicable set" rule. Neither document states this correspondence.</p> SQL used <p>Run against <code>solo-2025-09-16.sqlite</code> (and <code>solo-2020-04-16.sqlite</code> for the 2020 column).</p> <pre><code>SELECT OCP_TYPE, count(*) FROM ocp GROUP BY 1;
SELECT OCF_INTER, OCF_CODIN, count(*) FROM ocf GROUP BY 1,2;
SELECT OCF_NBOOL, count(*) FROM ocf GROUP BY 1;
SELECT OCF_NBCHCK, count(*) FROM ocf GROUP BY 1;
SELECT count(*) FROM ocp WHERE OCP_RLCHK &lt;&gt; '';
SELECT OCP_RLCHK, count(*) FROM ocp WHERE OCP_RLCHK &lt;&gt; '' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 6;

-- soft/hard pairs versus isolated limit checks (numerical sets only)
SELECT count(*) FROM (SELECT OCP_NAME, OCP_RLCHK, OCP_VALPAR FROM ocp o
   JOIN ocf f ON f.OCF_NAME = o.OCP_NAME
  WHERE f.OCF_CODIN &lt;&gt; 'A' AND OCP_TYPE IN ('S','H')
  GROUP BY 1,2,3 HAVING sum(OCP_TYPE='S') = 1 AND sum(OCP_TYPE='H') = 1);   -- 1,534

-- examples
SELECT * FROM ocp WHERE OCP_NAME IN ('NMUD1113','NRU02712','NCDT09N0','NIU01008')
 ORDER BY OCP_NAME, CAST(OCP_POS AS int);
SELECT OCP_LVALU, OCP_HVALU, count(*) FROM ocp WHERE OCP_TYPE = 'D' GROUP BY 1,2;  -- 0/0: 102, 2048/2048: 3
SELECT * FROM ocp WHERE OCP_TYPE = 'C';
SELECT * FROM cve WHERE COL2 IN (SELECT PCF_NAME FROM pcf WHERE PCF_USCON = 'Y');  -- none

-- consistency observations
SELECT f.* FROM ocf f WHERE CAST(OCF_NBOOL AS int) &lt;&gt;
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ocp WHERE OCP_NAME = f.OCF_NAME);                       -- NCDX9308
SELECT count(DISTINCT o.OCP_NAME) FROM ocp o JOIN pcf p ON p.PCF_NAME = o.OCP_NAME
 WHERE OCP_TYPE IN ('S','H') AND p.PCF_CATEG = 'S' AND o.OCP_HVALU &lt;&gt; '';         -- 349

-- on-board monitoring reports
SELECT PID_TYPE, PID_STYPE, PID_APID, PID_PI1_VAL, PID_SPID, PID_TPSD, PID_DESCR
  FROM pid WHERE PID_TYPE = '12';
</code></pre>

Table 2020-04-16 release 2025-09-16 release PCF 102,104 126,745 PID 9,027 11,150 PIC 228 256 PLF 326,492 400,792 VPD 18,770 19,863 GRP / GRPK 1 / 4 2 / 4 <p>Counts from the two public releases.<sup>54</sup><sup>66</sup> The 2020 release had 36 hard-coded synthetic parameters (<code>H</code>) that are gone in 2025. Dynamic OL parameters grew from 354 to 1,582 over the same period.<sup>55</sup><sup>66</sup></p> <p>All 36 of the 2020 <code>H</code> parameters still exist in the 2025 PCF, now with nature <code>D</code>. The 2020 release already shipped an OL definition file for each of them: its <code>synthetic</code> table holds 390 files, one for each of the 354 <code>D</code> and 36 <code>H</code> parameters.<sup>55</sup><sup>66</sup></p> <p>Inference</p> <p>The data suggest that the mission switched these 36 parameters from compiled to interpreted evaluation of the same OL definitions between the two releases. The MIB itself does not record the reason.</p> SQL used <p>Run against <code>solo-2025-09-16.sqlite</code> (and <code>solo-2020-04-16.sqlite</code> for the 2020 column). All columns are stored as text, hence the casts.</p> <pre><code>-- table sizes and database version
SELECT * FROM vdf;
SELECT count(*) FROM pcf;  -- likewise pid, pic, tpcf, plf, vpd, grp, grpa, grpk

-- PCF distributions
SELECT PCF_NATUR, count(*) FROM pcf GROUP BY 1;
SELECT PCF_CATEG, count(*) FROM pcf GROUP BY 1;
SELECT PCF_PTC,   count(*) FROM pcf GROUP BY 1;
SELECT PCF_VALPAR, count(*) FROM pcf WHERE PCF_VALID &lt;&gt; '' GROUP BY 1;
SELECT PCF_VALID, count(*) FROM pcf WHERE PCF_VALID &lt;&gt; '' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 8;
SELECT count(*) FROM pcf v WHERE v.PCF_NAME IN (SELECT PCF_VALID FROM pcf WHERE PCF_VALID &lt;&gt; '')
   AND CAST(v.PCF_PTC AS int) NOT IN (1,2,3,4);                       -- 0
SELECT PCF_NAME, PCF_DESCR, PCF_PTC, PCF_PFC, PCF_WIDTH, PCF_RELATED FROM pcf WHERE PCF_WIDTH &lt;&gt; '';

-- packet identification
SELECT PID_TYPE||','||PID_STYPE, PID_EVENT, count(*) FROM pid GROUP BY 1,2 ORDER BY 3 DESC;
SELECT PID_DFHSIZE, count(*) FROM pid GROUP BY 1;
SELECT * FROM pid WHERE CAST(PID_APID AS int) &gt; 2047 OR PID_CHECK = '1' OR PID_TIME = 'N';
SELECT PIC_PI1_OFF, PIC_PI1_WID, PIC_PI2_OFF, PIC_PI2_WID, count(*) FROM pic GROUP BY 1,2,3,4;
SELECT TPCF_SIZE = '0', count(*), min(CAST(TPCF_SIZE AS int)), max(CAST(TPCF_SIZE AS int))
  FROM tpcf GROUP BY 1;                                                 -- 2,325 zero / 8,825 non-zero
SELECT count(*) FROM pid WHERE PID_TPSD &lt;&gt; '-1' AND PID_TPSD &lt;&gt; PID_SPID;  -- 0

-- fixed packets
SELECT count(*) FROM plf WHERE CAST(PLF_NBOCC AS int) &gt; 1;
SELECT l.*, c.PCF_PTC, c.PCF_PFC FROM plf l JOIN pcf c ON c.PCF_NAME = l.PLF_NAME
